family: M0
corpus: C2
modes: bare-user-only
tier: T0
outcome: unobserved
severity: interop
identity: unobserved
oracle: exit-status checksum-agreement
evidence: -
spec: format-reference.md#repository-modes-and-on-disk-storage
question: This mode limits regular-file bits to 0775, so the setuid, setgid, and
 sticky bits have nowhere to live. Observe whether the tool refuses the commit,
 drops the bits silently, or stores them, and whether directories behave the same
 as regular files. The tool offers a --bareuseronly-files option on pull that
 rejects files with bits outside 0775, which suggests rejection is at least one
 available behavior.

family: M0
corpus: C3
modes: archive bare-user
tier: T0
outcome: full
severity: interop
identity: full
setup: repo tree
run: commit --repo=$REPO -b conformance -s x --timestamp=@1700000000 --owner-uid=42 --owner-gid=43 $TREE
expect-exit: 0
expect-stderr: empty
oracle: exit-status checksum-agreement inventory
spec: format-reference.md#commit
note: the declared ids reach every entry -- the tree root's own metadata, the
 nested directory, the regular files, and the symlink -- and both implementations
 reach one commit checksum for them. An unprivileged writer can record any ids in
 these two modes, since the ownership lives in the object header rather than on
 the object inode; the `bare` row below is the mode where it does not.

family: M0
corpus: C3
modes: bare-user-shared
tier: T0
outcome: full
severity: interop
identity: full
oracle: checksum-agreement
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::declared_ownership_is_one_commit_across_modes
spec: format-reference.md#port-extension-bare-user-shared-mode
note: the tool cannot open this mode, so the oracle is the cross-mode identity
 invariant rather than a direct comparison, as it is for C0: the cited test
 commits the declared ownership into archive, bare-user, and bare-user-shared and
 holds the three checksums to each other, and the archive and bare-user rows
 above compare theirs against the tool.

family: M0
corpus: C3
modes: bare-user-only
tier: T0
outcome: lossy
severity: interop
identity: full
setup: repo tree
run: commit --repo=$REPO -b conformance -s x --timestamp=@1700000000 --owner-uid=42 --owner-gid=43 $TREE
expect-exit: 0
expect-stderr: empty
oracle: exit-status checksum-agreement inventory
loss: The uid and gid are discarded and read back as 0. The mode stores no
 user.ostreemeta.
spec: format-reference.md#repository-modes-and-on-disk-storage
note: both halves observed. The commit checksum records the declared ids: it
 differs from the same tree committed without them, and the two implementations
 agree on it, so a pull into a checksum-preserving mode recovers what this mode
 does not store. The stored objects carry no ownership, so `ostree ls` reads 0 for
 both ids out of either implementation's repository. The identity this mode's
 reduction gives is its own: the checksum differs from the archive and bare-user
 rows above, which record the mode as well as the ids.

family: M0
corpus: C3
modes: bare
tier: T3
outcome: needs-priv
severity: interop
identity: unobserved
oracle: checksum-agreement exit-status
evidence: -
spec: format-reference.md#repository-modes-and-on-disk-storage
question: Confirm the two implementations agree on the commit checksum at real
 root, where the chown the ids need succeeds. The unprivileged half is observed:
 a non-root writer is refused by both at exit 1, leaving no ref and no object,
 the tool reporting `Writing content object: fchown: Operation not permitted` and
 the port `i/o error: Operation not permitted (os error 1)`. The refusal cannot be
 this cell's claim, since the same cell run at the tier it names must succeed.

